                    coach_r3_factory:       `<strong>A data-product factory is the repeatable engine.</strong> Sensing + a stage-gated pipeline + an industrialised build means product four arrives without another emergency CIO hire. You've funded <em>how</em> Meridian creates value, not just <em>what</em> it sells today — exactly Rosemann's point.`,
                    coach_r3_ambidextrous:  `<strong>That's ambidexterity done right.</strong> The factory builds the new while the core runs the profitable old — under separate governance and explore-stage metrics. The ventures will be judged on validated demand and learning, not the flagship's 18% margin, so they survive past month four.`,
                    coach_r3_noprocess:     `<strong>You funded products for today and no engine for tomorrow.</strong> When the next disruption hits in 2028, what produces product four? Without an explorative process pattern you're back here in two years with another emergency hire. Rosemann's whole point is the repeatable engine, not the one-off offering.`,
                    coach_r3_coregov:       `<strong>Judging the ventures on the cash cow's 18% margin and quarterly targets will kill them by month four.</strong> A two-year-old venture can't hit the metrics of a thirty-year-old cash cow — and it shouldn't have to. Govern explore on validated demand and learning, or the core's logic strangles every new stream.`,
                    coach_r3_nogov:         `<strong>No stage gates and no metrics isn't ambidexterity — it's abdication.</strong> Explore needs <em>different</em> governance, not <em>no</em> governance. Without leading indicators you can't tell a venture that's learning from one that's just burning cash.`,
                    coach_r3_revenueonly:   `<strong>“Billing within two quarters or kill it” is the core's yardstick in disguise.</strong> New streams take longer than that to reach revenue — judge them on it and you'll cut the winners before they validate. Watch the leading indicators, not just the invoices.`,
