                    insight_resilient:      `Resilience built: you named stream concentration as the structural risk, funded a staggered portfolio rather than a single bet, cannibalised the flagship on your own terms, institutionalised a repeatable explore engine under separate governance, and hedged the residual exposure. Single-stream dependence fell below 60%, new revenue compounds, and Meridian can now produce its next stream without another emergency hire.`,
                    insight_polish:         `Polished the cash cow: you spent the budget defending the 74% stream a free AI tool is already eating. The flagship is shinier, but resilience barely moved and dependence stayed in the danger zone — you made the brittle thing more attractive instead of building the second stream that survives if it falls. This is the headline error the cycle was designed to break.`,
                    insight_single:         `One bet, new concentration: a single new product became the whole resilience answer, swapping one single-stream risk for another. If that bet is late or out-executed, you are back where you started. Resilience is a portfolio with different timings and risk profiles — not a hero replacement.`,
                    insight_noprocess:      `Products, no engine: you funded offerings for today but no explorative process pattern to generate tomorrow's. When the next disruption lands in 2028, nothing produces product four — and the company is back here with another emergency CIO. Resilience is a repeatable engine, not a one-off product slate.`,
                    insight_onesize:        `Ventures strangled: judging the new streams on the cash cow's 18% margin and quarterly targets killed them on the wrong yardstick. A young venture can't be measured like a thirty-year cash cow — without ambidextrous governance and explore-stage metrics, the core's logic quietly suffocates the new.`,
                    insight_paralysis:      `Cannibalisation paralysis: you refused to build the AI assistant to protect Sector Reports — and so left that revenue for DataLive and the free tools to take instead. The cash cow gets cannibalised either way; declining to do it on your own terms simply handed the disruption a clear run.`,
                    insight_nohedge:        `Unhedged: the plan assumed you could out-innovate generative AI on every front, with no partnership, acquisition or lock-in as insurance. Resilient firms hedge the downside they cannot out-build — one wrong bet now unravels the whole plan because nothing backs it up.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed cycle: some of the resilience work landed, but the portfolio, the explore engine, the governance and the hedges didn't fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for the board to doubt whether dependence really falls below 60% and stays there.`,
                    insight_dep_high:       `Single-stream dependence never cleared the 60% mandate: too much revenue still sits in the flagship, so the board's core test — a credible path below 60% in 24 months — was not met, whatever else the plan achieved.`,
                    insight_misdiagnosed:   `The diagnosis was off: you didn't name stream concentration as the structural brittleness driver in Round 1, so the cycle was built on the wrong foundation — fixing symptoms rather than the single-stream risk itself.`,
                    insight_thin_newrev:    `New-stream contribution stayed thin: the streams you funded never compounded into enough new revenue to materially diversify the base, so dependence relief leaned on shrinking the flagship rather than growing alternatives.`,
                    insight_roi_low:        `Innovation ROI came in below 1×: you committed capital faster than the streams returned projected revenue. Resilience has to be funded efficiently — the board will ask why the spend outran the new revenue it bought.`,
                    insight_exposure_left:  `Dependence fell but disruption exposure is still high: too much revenue remains in the direct path of AI and API disruption. Reducing concentration without hedging the exposed revenue leaves the plan one bad quarter from a relapse.`,

                    hint_polish:            `You're polishing the cash cow — a nicer flagship is still one brittle stream. Pivot the budget into a portfolio of new streams that survive if Sector Reports falls 15%.`,
                    hint_single:            `One product isn't diversification — it's a new concentration. Add a second and third stream with different timings before the plan rests on a single bet.`,
                    hint_noprocess:         `You funded products but no engine. Institutionalise an explorative process pattern, or you'll be back here in 2028 with nothing to produce product four.`,
                    hint_onesize:           `Your ventures are being judged on the cash cow's margin and quarterly targets — they'll be killed early. Govern explore separately, on validated-demand metrics.`,
